# =============================================================================
#  LISFLOOD-FP 8.4 example parameter file
# =============================================================================
#  Run it from this folder:
#      ../build/lisflood example.par
#  It simulates a 3 h storm on a synthetic valley (800 m x 1200 m, 10 m cells)
#  and takes a few seconds on a GPU.
#
#  How this file is read:
#  - One keyword per line, followed by its value if it takes one.
#  - A line that starts with # is ignored. Text after a value is also ignored,
#    so a short note can follow the value on the same line.
#  - Keywords are not case sensitive. Paths are relative to the folder you
#    run lisflood from.
#  - Times are in seconds, lengths in metres, rain and soil rates in mm/h
#    (unless a line says otherwise).
# =============================================================================


# --- 1. Input files ----------------------------------------------------------

# Ground elevation, ESRI ASCII grid (.asc). It sets the domain, the cell size
# and the coordinates that every other input uses.
DEMfile         dem.asc

# Roughness: one Manning n for the whole domain (fpfric), or a grid of n with
# the same size as the DEM (manningfile). Use one of the two.
fpfric          0.035
#manningfile    manning.asc

# Boundary conditions on the domain edges (.bci). This example has one line,
# "S 0 800 FREE": the south edge from x = 0 to x = 800 m lets water out.
# The first field is the edge (N, S, E or W), then the start and end
# coordinate along it, then the type:
#     FREE          open edge: water flows out freely. In 8.4 FREE is outflow
#                   only: it never pulls water into the domain. In fv1, fv2
#                   and dg2 it passes moving water; water at rest against
#                   the edge stays, which is why this valley keeps a pond
#                   at its outlet (acceleration drains it).
#     HFIX  2.5     fixed water surface elevation (m)
#     QFIX  1.0     fixed inflow per metre of edge (m2/s)
#     HVAR  name    elevation time series from bdyfile
#     QVAR  name    inflow time series from bdyfile
# Edges that are not listed are closed walls. "P x y QVAR name" adds a
# point source at (x, y).
bcifile         boundary.bci
#bdyfile        boundary.bdy   # time series named in the .bci (HVAR/QVAR)

# Initial water depth (ASCII grid in m). Without it the domain starts dry.
#startfile      start.wd


# --- 2. Rain -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Uniform rain: a time series in mm/h, the same over the whole domain.
# File layout (see rain.txt):
#     line 1      a comment
#     line 2      number of rows, then seconds, minutes or hours
#     then        one "rate time" pair per row, linearly interpolated
# After the last row the rain is 0.
rainfall        rain.txt

# Or rain that varies in space and time, from a NetCDF file on the DEM grid:
#dynamicrainfile rain.nc
# Use only one of the two.


# --- 3. Infiltration and evaporation (new in 8.4, on every GPU solver) -------

# Pick ONE infiltration model. Every soil value can be one number or the name
# of an ASCII grid with the same size as the DEM.

# (a) Green-Ampt
infiltration         greenampt
greenampt_ks         5       # saturated hydraulic conductivity (mm/h)
greenampt_psi        26.7    # wetting-front suction head (mm)
greenampt_porosity   0.40    # porosity (-)
greenampt_theta_init 0.30    # initial water content (-), below the porosity
#greenampt_soil_depth 1.0    # optional: soil depth (m); infiltration stops when full

# (b) Horton: f(t) = fc + (f0 - fc) exp(-k t)
#infiltration   horton
#horton_f0      60           # initial capacity (mm/h)
#horton_fc      10           # final capacity (mm/h)
#horton_k       2            # decay constant (1/h)

# (c) Constant rate, in m/s, or a grid of rates in mm/h
#infiltration   1e-6
#infilfile      rates.asc

# Evaporation: a time series in mm/day. It can be combined with any of the
# above.
#evaporation    evap.dat


# --- 4. Solver ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Choose one of:
#   fv1           first-order finite volume (shallow water). Fast and robust.
#                 The usual first choice.
#   fv2           second-order finite volume
#   dg2           second-order discontinuous Galerkin, the most accurate. It
#                 needs slope files dem.asc1x and dem.asc1y next to the DEM.
#                 Make them with:  generateDG2DEM example.par
#                 That command OVERWRITES dem.asc, so run it on a copy.
#   acceleration  local inertial (ACC). The fastest; for floodplains and
#                 slowly varying flow, not for steep terrain.
#   acc_nugrid    ACC on a non-uniform grid
#   hwfv1         adaptive FV1 (multiresolution)
#   mwdg2         adaptive DG2 (multiresolution)
#                 On CUDA, hwfv1 and mwdg2 take no rain (Metal does); give them
#                 water through startfile or the boundary conditions instead.
fv1

# acc_nugrid, hwfv1 and mwdg2 work on a square grid of 2^L x 2^L cells that
# must cover the DEM: L = 7 gives 128, enough for this 80 x 120 DEM.
# The other solvers ignore it.
max_ref_lvl     7

# Run on the GPU. "cuda" and "metal" are the same switch: the build picks
# NVIDIA (CUDA) or Apple Silicon (Metal) itself. Without it the run is on
# the CPU: much slower, and the CPU fv1 and dg2 take rain only from
# dynamicrainfile.
cuda

# Several GPUs (fv1, fv2 and dg2; the build needs MPI). Start the run with
#     mpirun -np 4 ../build/lisflood example.par
# where -np equals the value below. Each GPU takes a band of DEM rows.
# For fv2 and dg2, give each rank its own GPU:
#     mpirun -np 4 ../tests_8.4/multigpu/rank_gpu.sh ../build/lisflood example.par
#GPUs           4

# Optional solver settings. The defaults suit most cases. Put them AFTER the
# solver keyword above, which sets its own defaults.
#cfl            0.5          # Courant number (default fv1 0.5, fv2 0.33, acceleration 0.7)
#depththresh    1e-3         # depth (m) below which a cell counts as dry
#epsilon        1e-3         # hwfv1/mwdg2: error threshold for mesh adaptation
#limitslopes                 # fv2/dg2/mwdg2: slope limiter for steep fronts


# --- 5. Time -----------------------------------------------------------------

sim_time        10800        # length of the simulation (s): 3 h
initial_tstep   10           # LARGEST time step allowed (s), also the first one;
                             # the solver takes smaller steps when the CFL needs them
massint         60           # interval of the mass-balance record (s)
saveint         1800         # interval of the output grids (s)


# --- 6. Output ---------------------------------------------------------------

dirroot         results      # output folder (created if missing)
resroot         example      # prefix of every output file

# What is written, for resroot "example":
#   example.mass           every massint: the water budget (time, time step,
#                          wet area, volume, inflow Qin, outflow Qout, the
#                          volume error Verror since the last line and its
#                          rate Qerror, and rain minus infiltration and
#                          evaporation since the start)
#   example-0001.wd ...    water depth (m) every saveint
#   example-0001.elev ...  water surface elevation (m) every saveint
#   example.max            maximum depth over the whole run
#   example.stage          depth (m) at the points of the stage file, every massint
stagefile       gauges.stage # first line: number of points; then "x y" per point

# Turn outputs off or add more:
elevoff                      # do not write .elev grids
#depthoff                    # do not write .wd grids
#voutput                     # also write velocity grids (.Vx, .Vy) every saveint
#netcdf_out                  # write the grids as NetCDF instead of ASCII


# --- 7. DEM NoData cells -----------------------------------------------------

# Cells with the DEM NODATA value (-9999 here) lie outside the catchment.
# These two lines make them a low sink that removes the water reaching them,
# so a catchment cut out of a larger DEM drains at its edge.
#nodata_elevation -9999
#drain_nodata
